            "summary": "### Core Concepts\nAngular displacement is a measure of the change in the angular position of an object from its initial position to its final position. It is a scalar quantity and is measured in radians. The angular displacement is given by the equation:\n\n$$\\theta = \\theta_f - \\theta_i$$\n\nwhere $\\theta_i$ is the initial angular position and $\\theta_f$ is the final angular position.\n\nAngular velocity is a measure of the rate of change of angular displacement with respect to time. It is a vector quantity and is measured in radians per second. The angular velocity is given by the equation:\n\n$$\\omega = \\frac{d\\theta}{dt}$$\n\nwhere $\\omega$ is the angular velocity and $t$ is time.\n\nAngular velocity can also be expressed in terms of the linear velocity and the radius of the circular path:\n\n$$\\omega = \\frac{v}{r}$$\n\nwhere $v$ is the linear velocity and $r$ is the radius of the circular path.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\nImagine a merry-go-round that is rotating at a constant speed. If you start at the center of the merry-go-round and move to the edge, you will experience a change in your angular position. The angular displacement is a measure of this change. If you were to move around the merry-go-round in a circular path, your angular velocity would be constant.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\nThe angular velocity can be derived from the linear velocity using the following equation:\n\n$$\\omega = \\frac{v}{r} = \\frac{d\\theta}{dt}$$\n\nwhere $v$ is the linear velocity, $r$ is the radius of the circular path, and $t$ is time.\n\nTo derive this equation, we can start by considering the relationship between the linear velocity and the angular velocity:\n\n$$v = r\\omega$$\n\nwhere $v$ is the linear velocity, $r$ is the radius of the circular path, and $\\omega$ is the angular velocity.\n\nWe can then take the derivative of both sides of this equation with respect to time to get:\n\n$$\\frac{dv}{dt} = r\\frac{d\\omega}{dt}$$\n\nSince the linear velocity is constant, the derivative of the linear velocity with respect to time is zero:\n\n$$\\frac{dv}{dt} = 0$$\n\nSubstituting this into the previous equation, we get:\n\n$$0 = r\\frac{d\\omega}{dt}$$\n\nDividing both sides of this equation by $r$, we get:\n\n$$\\frac{d\\omega}{dt} = 0$$\n\nThis equation implies that the angular velocity is constant.\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\nWhen solving problems involving angular displacement and angular velocity, make sure to use the correct units. Angular displacement is measured in radians, while angular velocity is measured in radians per second.\n\nWhen using the equation $\\omega = \\frac{v}{r}$, make sure to use the correct values for the linear velocity and the radius of the circular path.\n\nWhen solving problems involving rotational motion, make sure to use the correct equations and formulas. The equation $\\omega = \\frac{d\\theta}{dt}$ is a fundamental equation in rotational motion, and it should be used whenever possible.\n\nIn addition to using the correct equations and formulas, make sure to use the correct units and to follow the correct order of operations. This will help you to avoid common pitfalls and to get the correct answer."
