            "summary": "### Core Concepts\n1. **Linear and Angular Displacement Relationship**: The arc length $s$ traveled along a circle of radius $r$ corresponds to an angular displacement $\\theta$ according to:\n   $$s = r\\theta$$\n2. **Linear and Angular Velocity Relationship**: The tangential velocity $v$ of a particle moving in a circular path is related to its angular velocity $\\omega$ by:\n   $$v = r\\omega$$\n   In vector notation, this cross product is:\n   $$\\vec{v} = \\vec{\\omega} \\times \\vec{r}$$\n3. **Linear and Angular Acceleration Relationship**: The tangential acceleration $a_t$ is related to angular acceleration $\\alpha$ by:\n   $$a_t = r\\alpha$$\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\nImagine a merry-go-round with a group of children sitting on it. As the merry-go-round rotates, each child moves in a circular path. A child sitting near the center pivot moves in a small circle, while a child sitting near the outer edge sweeps out a much larger circle. Even though both children complete one full rotation in the exact same time (possessing identical angular velocity $\\omega$), the child on the edge travels a much larger physical arc distance and feels a stronger rush of wind because their linear speed ($v = r\\omega$) is scaled up by their larger radius.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\nTo derive the velocity relation, we begin with the scalar definition of arc length:\n$$s = r\\theta$$\nSince the radius $r$ is constant for a circular path, differentiating both sides with respect to time $t$ yields:\n$$\\frac{ds}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt}(r\\theta) = r\\frac{d\\theta}{dt}$$\nBy definition, $\\frac{ds}{dt} = v$ (tangential speed) and $\\frac{d\\theta}{dt} = \\omega$ (angular velocity). Substituting these definitions gives:\n$$v = r\\omega$$\n\nDifferentiating the velocity relation once more with respect to time gives the tangential acceleration relation:\n$$a_t = \\frac{dv}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt}(r\\omega) = r\\frac{d\\omega}{dt}$$\nSince $\\frac{d\\omega}{dt} = \\alpha$ (angular acceleration), we obtain:\n$$a_t = r\\alpha$$\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\n- **Total Acceleration Check**: In non-uniform circular motion, the total linear acceleration is the vector sum of tangential acceleration ($a_t = r\\alpha$) and centripetal acceleration ($a_c = v^2/r = \\omega^2 r$):\n  $$a_{total} = \\sqrt{a_t^2 + a_c^2}$$\n- **Vector Cross Products**: Remember that $\\vec{v} = \\vec{\\omega} \\times \\vec{r}$. Reversing the cross product order to $\\vec{r} \\times \\vec{\\omega}$ is a common sign error on exams!"
