            "id": "rotational_motion_4_3",
            "title": "Rotational Motion and Gravitational Potential Energy",
            "topic": "Rotational Motion and Gravitation",
            "duration": 45,
            "summary": "### Core Concepts\n\nRotational motion and gravitational potential energy are closely related concepts in physics. The gravitational potential energy of an object is defined as the energy it possesses due to its position in a gravitational field. In the context of rotational motion, the gravitational potential energy of an object can be expressed as:\n\n$$U_g = -\\frac{GMm}{r}$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $M$ is the mass of the central body (e.g. Earth), $m$ is the mass of the object, and $r$ is the distance between the object and the center of the central body.\n\nThe rotational kinetic energy of an object is given by:\n\n$$K_r = \\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the moment of inertia of the object and $\\omega$ is its angular velocity.\n\nThe moment of inertia of an object depends on its mass distribution and can be calculated using the following equation:\n\n$$I = \\int r^2 dm$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the distance from the axis of rotation to the infinitesimal mass element $dm$.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\n\nImagine a ball rolling down a hill. As the ball rolls, its potential energy (due to its height) is converted into kinetic energy (due to its motion). In the context of rotational motion, the ball's potential energy is converted into rotational kinetic energy as it rolls down the hill.\n\nThe analogy can be extended to a person standing on the surface of the Earth. The person's weight is a result of the gravitational force exerted by the Earth, which is a manifestation of the Earth's gravitational potential energy. As the person moves closer to the center of the Earth, their weight increases due to the increase in gravitational potential energy.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\n\nThe gravitational potential energy of an object can be derived from the work done by the gravitational force in moving the object from a reference point to a point at a distance $r$ from the center of the central body. The work done is given by:\n\n$$W = -\\int_{r_0}^r \\frac{GMm}{r^2} dr$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we get:\n\n$$W = -\\frac{GMm}{r} + \\frac{GMm}{r_0}$$\n\nThe work done is equal to the change in gravitational potential energy, so we can write:\n\n$$\\Delta U_g = -\\frac{GMm}{r} + \\frac{GMm}{r_0}$$\n\nThe rotational kinetic energy of an object can be derived from the conservation of energy principle. The total energy of the object is conserved, so we can write:\n\n$$E_{\\text{total}} = U_g + K_r$$\n\nSubstituting the expressions for $U_g$ and $K_r$, we get:\n\n$$E_{\\text{total}} = -\\frac{GMm}{r} + \\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2$$\n\nThe moment of inertia of an object can be calculated using the following equation:\n\n$$I = \\int r^2 dm$$\n\nFor a solid sphere of radius $R$ and mass $M$, the moment of inertia is given by:\n\n$$I = \\frac{2}{5}MR^2$$\n\nFor a hollow sphere of radius $R$ and mass $M$, the moment of inertia is given by:\n\n$$I = \\frac{2}{3}MR^2$$\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\n\nWhen solving problems involving rotational motion and gravitational potential energy, make sure to:\n\n* Use the correct equation for gravitational potential energy: $U_g = -\\frac{GMm}{r}$\n* Use the correct equation for rotational kinetic energy: $K_r = \\frac{1}{2}"
