            "summary": "### Core Concepts\nOrganic chemistry is the branch of chemistry that deals with the study of carbon-containing compounds. These compounds can be found in living organisms and are the basis of life. The core concept of organic chemistry is the ability of carbon atoms to form long chains and rings, which allows for the creation of a vast array of complex molecules.\n\nThe key to understanding organic chemistry is the concept of hybridization, which describes the mixing of atomic orbitals to form new orbitals. In the case of carbon, the hybridization of 2s and 3p orbitals forms four equivalent sp^3 orbitals, allowing carbon to form four bonds. This is in contrast to the hybridization of 2s and 2p orbitals in nitrogen, which forms three equivalent sp^2 orbitals.\n\nThe IUPAC nomenclature system is used to name organic compounds. This system is based on the source of the compound, the functional group present, and the longest continuous chain of carbon atoms. For example, the compound CH_3CH_2OH is named ethanol, with the prefix 'eth-' indicating that it is derived from ethane, and the suffix '-ol' indicating the presence of a hydroxyl functional group.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\nImagine a LEGO set, where each LEGO brick represents a carbon atom. The bricks can be connected in various ways to form different structures, just like how carbon atoms can form different bonds to create a wide range of molecules. The LEGO bricks can also be linked together to form long chains or rings, just like how carbon atoms can form long chains or rings in organic compounds.\n\nIn this analogy, the LEGO bricks can be thought of as the 'building blocks' of organic chemistry. Just as the LEGO bricks can be combined in different ways to create different structures, carbon atoms can be combined in different ways to create a vast array of complex molecules. This analogy helps to illustrate the concept of hybridization, where the LEGO bricks can be mixed and matched to form new structures.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\nThe hybridization of carbon atoms can be described using the following equation:\n\n$$\\psi = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left( \\psi_{2s} + \\psi_{2p} \\right)$$\n\nwhere $\\psi$ is the hybridized orbital, $\\psi_{2s}$ is the 2s orbital, and $\\psi_{2p}$ is the 2p orbital. This equation shows that the hybridized orbital is a combination of the 2s and 2p orbitals.\n\nThe IUPAC nomenclature system can be derived from the following rules:\n\n* The prefix is derived from the source of the compound, with the following prefixes being commonly used:\n + 'meth-' for compounds derived from methane\n + 'eth-' for compounds derived from ethane\n + 'prop-' for compounds derived from propane\n* The suffix is derived from the functional group present, with the following suffixes being commonly used:\n + '-ol' for compounds containing a hydroxyl functional group\n + '-al' for compounds containing an aldehyde functional group\n + '-oic acid' for compounds containing a carboxyl functional group\n\nThe following equation shows how the IUPAC nomenclature system can be used to name a compound:\n\n$$\\text{Compound name} = \\text{prefix} + \\text{root} + \\text{suffix}"
