            "topic": "History and Development of Organic Chemistry",
            "duration": 25,
            "summary": "### Core Concepts\nOrganic chemistry is a branch of chemistry that deals with the study of carbon-containing compounds. The term 'organic' was initially used to describe compounds that were derived from living organisms. However, with the discovery of synthetic methods for producing organic compounds, the definition of organic chemistry has been expanded to include all carbon-containing compounds, regardless of their origin.\n\nKey concepts in organic chemistry include functional groups, isomerism, and stereochemistry. Functional groups are specific groups of atoms within a molecule that determine its chemical properties. Isomerism refers to the existence of molecules with the same molecular formula but different structural formulas. Stereochemistry deals with the three-dimensional arrangement of atoms in a molecule.\n\n$$C_nH_{2n+2}$$ is the general formula for alkanes, where n is the number of carbon atoms. Alkanes are saturated hydrocarbons, meaning they contain only single bonds between carbon atoms.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\nImagine a city as a molecule. The streets and roads represent the bonds between atoms, and the buildings and houses represent the functional groups. Just as a city can have different layouts and designs, molecules can have different structures and arrangements of functional groups. This analogy helps to illustrate the concept of isomerism and the importance of understanding the three-dimensional arrangement of atoms in a molecule.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\nThe discovery of organic chemistry can be attributed to the work of Antoine Lavoisier, who is considered the 'father of modern chemistry'. Lavoisier discovered the elements oxygen and hydrogen and demonstrated that combustion reactions involve the combination of oxygen with other elements. His work laid the foundation for the development of organic chemistry.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\text{Combustion reaction:} \\quad C + O_2 &\\rightarrow CO_2 \\\n\\text{Oxidation reaction:} \\quad 2H_2 + O_2 &\\rightarrow 2H_2O\n\\end{align*}"
