            "topic": "Basic Concepts and Terminology",
            "duration": 30,
            "summary": "### Core Concepts\nIn organic chemistry, the core concepts revolve around the study of carbon-based compounds. The most fundamental concept is the **tetravalency of carbon**, which states that carbon atoms can form four bonds with other atoms. This property allows carbon to form a wide variety of molecules with different structures and properties.\n\nThe **saturated and unsaturated hydrocarbons** are two types of hydrocarbons that differ in the number of hydrogen atoms bonded to the carbon atoms. Saturated hydrocarbons have only single bonds between carbon atoms and are also known as alkanes. Unsaturated hydrocarbons have one or more multiple bonds between carbon atoms and are classified into alkenes and alkynes.\n\nThe **isomerism** concept is also crucial in organic chemistry. Isomers are molecules that have the same molecular formula but differ in their structural arrangement of atoms. There are several types of isomerism, including structural isomerism, stereoisomerism, and conformational isomerism.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\nImagine a LEGO brick as a carbon atom. Each LEGO brick can connect to four other bricks, just like carbon can form four bonds with other atoms. The different arrangements of LEGO bricks can represent different molecules, just like how different structures of carbon atoms form different molecules. This analogy helps to illustrate the concept of isomerism, where different arrangements of LEGO bricks can result in different structures.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\nThe **hybridization of atomic orbitals** is a key concept in understanding the bonding in organic molecules. According to the VSEPR theory, the hybridization of atomic orbitals determines the shape of the molecule. For example, the sp^3 hybridization results in a tetrahedral shape, while the sp^2 hybridization results in a trigonal planar shape.\n\nThe **bonding and antibonding molecular orbitals** are also crucial in understanding the electronic structure of molecules. The bonding molecular orbitals are formed by the overlap of atomic orbitals with the same sign, while the antibonding molecular orbitals are formed by the overlap of atomic orbitals with opposite signs. The energy of the molecular orbitals is determined by the energy of the atomic orbitals and the overlap between them.\n\n$$\\Psi = \\psi_1 \\psi_2 \\psi_3 \\psi_4$$\n\n$$E = \\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m} \\left( \\frac{1}{\\psi_1^2} + \\frac{1}{\\psi_2^2} + \\frac{1}{\\psi_3^2} + \\frac{1}{\\psi_4^2}"
