            "topic": "Types of Bonds in Organic Compounds",
            "duration": 30,
            "summary": "### Core Concepts\nIn organic chemistry, bonds are formed between atoms due to the sharing of electrons. There are several types of bonds, including covalent bonds, ionic bonds, and metallic bonds. Covalent bonds are formed when two or more atoms share one or more pairs of electrons to achieve a stable electronic configuration. This type of bonding is typically found in molecules.\n\n$$\\text{Bond order} = \\frac{\\text{Number of bonding electrons} - \\text{Number of antibonding electrons}}{2}$$\n\nCovalent bonds can be further classified into single, double, and triple bonds based on the number of electron pairs shared between the atoms. Single bonds involve the sharing of one pair of electrons, double bonds involve the sharing of two pairs of electrons, and triple bonds involve the sharing of three pairs of electrons.\n\nIonic bonds, on the other hand, are formed between two atoms with a large difference in electronegativity. In this type of bonding, one atom loses an electron to form a cation, while the other atom gains an electron to form an anion. The electrostatic attraction between the oppositely charged ions holds them together.\n\nMetallic bonds are formed in metals and involve the delocalization of electrons among a lattice of metal ions. This type of bonding is responsible for the high electrical conductivity and malleability of metals.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\nImagine a group of friends who want to start a business together. They need to work together to achieve their goals, but they also need to respect each other's differences and opinions. In this scenario, the friends can be thought of as atoms, and their relationships can be thought of as bonds. Just as the friends need to work together to achieve their goals, atoms need to form bonds to achieve a stable electronic configuration.\n\nIn the case of covalent bonds, the friends can be thought of as sharing resources, such as money or expertise, to achieve a common goal. This is similar to how atoms share electrons to form a covalent bond. In the case of ionic bonds, one friend can be thought of as taking on a leadership role, while the other friend follows their lead. This is similar to how one atom loses an electron to form a cation, while the other atom gains an electron to form an anion.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\nThe strength of a covalent bond can be calculated using the following equation:\n\n$$E = -\\frac{n}{r}$$\n\nwhere $E$ is the bond energy, $n$ is the number of electrons shared, and $r$ is the distance between the nuclei of the two atoms.\n\nTo derive this equation, we can start by considering the energy of a single electron in a molecule. The energy of an electron in a molecule can be calculated using the following equation:\n\n$$E = -\\frac{1}{r}$$\n\nSince there are $n$ electrons shared in a covalent bond, the total energy of the bond can be calculated by summing the energies of each electron:\n\n$$E = -\\frac{n}{r}$$\n\nThis equation shows that the strength of a covalent bond is inversely proportional to the distance between the nuclei of the two atoms.\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\nWhen solving exam problems involving covalent bonds, it's essential to remember that the strength of a bond is determined by the number of electrons shared and the distance between the nuclei of the two atoms. You can use the equation $E = -\\frac{n}{r}"
