            "summary": "### Core Concepts\nOrganic reactions are chemical transformations that involve the breaking and forming of chemical bonds between atoms in organic compounds. These reactions can be broadly classified into several types based on the mechanism of the reaction, the type of bond formed or broken, and the conditions under which the reaction occurs.\n\n*   **Electrophilic Addition Reactions**: In these reactions, an electrophile (a species that is electron-deficient) adds to a multiple bond (such as a double or triple bond) in an organic compound. This results in the formation of a new bond between the electrophile and the organic compound. Examples of electrophilic addition reactions include the addition of hydrogen bromide (HBr) to alkenes and the addition of water to alkenes.\n\n$$\n\\text{RCH} = \\text{CH}_2 + \\text{HBr} \\rightarrow \\text{RCH}(\\text{Br})\\text{CH}_3\n$$\n\n*   **Nucleophilic Substitution Reactions**: In these reactions, a nucleophile (a species that is electron-rich) replaces a leaving group (a species that can leave a molecule) in an organic compound. This results in the formation of a new bond between the nucleophile and the organic compound. Examples of nucleophilic substitution reactions include the reaction of sodium hydroxide (NaOH) with methyl bromide (CH3Br) to form methanol (CH3OH) and sodium bromide (NaBr).\n\n$$\n\\text{CH}_3\\text{Br} + \\text{NaOH} \\rightarrow \\text{CH}_3\\text{OH} + \\text{NaBr}\n$$\n\n*   **Elimination Reactions**: In these reactions, a leaving group is removed from an organic compound, resulting in the formation of a new bond between the atoms that were previously bonded to the leaving group. Examples of elimination reactions include the reaction of sodium hydroxide (NaOH) with methyl chloride (CH3Cl) to form ethene (C2H4) and sodium chloride (NaCl).\n\n$$\n\\text{CH}_3\\text{Cl} + \\text{NaOH} \\rightarrow \\text{C}_2\\text{H}_4 + \\text{NaCl} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}\n$$\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\nImagine a city with a busy highway system. The highway system can be thought of as the bonds between atoms in an organic compound. Just as cars can travel along the highway, molecules can travel along the bonds between atoms. In an organic reaction, the highway system is modified by the addition or removal of cars (molecules). This can result in a change in the traffic flow (the reactivity of the molecule).\n\nFor example, consider a highway with a traffic light at a busy intersection. The traffic light can be thought of as a bond between two atoms. If the traffic light is red, it means that the bond is broken, and the cars (molecules) cannot travel along that highway. If the traffic light is green, it means that the bond is intact, and the cars can travel along that highway.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\nThe mechanism of an organic reaction can be understood by considering the energy changes that occur during the reaction. The energy change can be calculated using the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\Delta E = E_{\\text{products}} - E_{\\text{reactants}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta E$ is the energy change, $E_{\\text{products}}$ is the energy of the products, and $E_{\\text{reactants}}$ is the energy of the reactants.\n\nFor example, consider the reaction of hydrogen gas (H2) with oxygen gas (O2) to form water (H2O). The energy change for this reaction can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{align*}\n\\Delta E &= E_{\\text{products}} - E_{\\text{reactants}} \\\n&= E_{\\text{H}_2\\text{O}} - (E_{\\text{H}_2} + E_{\\text{O}_2}) \\\n&= -285.8 \\text{ kJ/mol} - (-436.0 \\text{ kJ/mol} + 0 \\text{ kJ/mol}) \\\n&= -285.8 \\text{ kJ/mol} + 436.0 \\text{ kJ/mol} \\\n&= 150.2 \\text{ kJ/mol}\n\\end{align*}\n$$\n\nThis means that the reaction of hydrogen gas with oxygen gas is exothermic, meaning that it releases energy.\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\nWhen answering exam questions on organic reactions, it is essential to understand the mechanism of the reaction and the energy changes that occur during the reaction. The following tips can help you to answer exam questions on organic reactions:\n\n*   **Understand the mechanism of the reaction**: The mechanism of the reaction is the sequence of steps that occur during the reaction. It is essential to understand the mechanism of the reaction to answer exam questions.\n*   **Calculate the energy change**: The energy change can be calculated using the equation $\\Delta E = E_{\\text{products}} - E_{\\text{reactants}}"
