            "summary": "### Core Concepts\n\nIn this section, we will discuss the core concepts of types of integrals. The two main types of integrals are definite integrals and indefinite integrals.\n\n*   **Definite Integrals**: A definite integral is a type of integral that has a specific upper and lower limit. It is denoted by $$\\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\, dx$$ and is used to find the area under a curve between two points $a$ and $b$. The definite integral can be calculated using the fundamental theorem of calculus, which states that $$\\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\, dx = F(b) - F(a)$$, where $F(x)$ is the antiderivative of $f(x)$.\n\n*   **Indefinite Integrals**: An indefinite integral is a type of integral that does not have a specific upper and lower limit. It is denoted by $$\\int f(x) \\, dx$$ and is used to find the antiderivative of a function. The indefinite integral can be calculated using various techniques such as substitution, integration by parts, and integration by partial fractions.\n\n*   **Improper Integrals**: An improper integral is a type of integral that has an infinite upper or lower limit. It is denoted by $$\\int_{a}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, dx$$ or $$\\int_{-\\infty}^{b} f(x) \\, dx$$ and is used to find the area under a curve that extends to infinity. The improper integral can be calculated using various techniques such as integration by parts and integration by partial fractions.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\n\nImagine you are a manager of a company that sells a product. You want to find the total revenue generated by the product over a certain period of time. The revenue is represented by the area under a curve that shows the sales of the product over time. The definite integral can be used to find the area under the curve, which represents the total revenue generated by the product.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\n\n**Derivation of Definite Integral**\n\nLet $f(x)$ be a continuous function on the interval $[a, b]$. We can divide the interval $[a, b]$ into $n$ subintervals of equal width $\\Delta x$. The area under the curve can be approximated by the sum of the areas of the rectangles formed by the function values at the endpoints of each subinterval.\n\n$$A \\approx \\sum_{i=1}^{n} f(x_i) \\Delta x$$\n\nAs $n$ approaches infinity, the approximation becomes more accurate, and the sum approaches the definite integral.\n\n$$\\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\, dx = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} f(x_i) \\Delta x$$\n\n**Derivation of Indefinite Integral**\n\nLet $f(x)$ be a continuous function on the interval $[a, b]$. We can define the antiderivative of $f(x)$ as the function $F(x)$ such that $$F'(x) = f(x)$$.\n\nUsing the fundamental theorem of calculus, we can show that the antiderivative of $f(x)$ is given by $$F(x) = \\int f(x) \\, dx$$.\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\n\n*   **Common Exam Pitfalls**: One common pitfall in exam questions is to confuse the definite integral with the indefinite integral. Make sure to check the limits of integration carefully to avoid this mistake.\n\n*   **Critical Shortcuts**: One critical shortcut in exam questions is to use the fundamental theorem of calculus to evaluate definite integrals. This can save a lot of time and effort.\n\n*   **Detailed Problem-Solving Tips**: When solving exam questions, make sure to read the question carefully and identify the type of integral required. Use the appropriate techniques such as substitution, integration by parts, and integration by partial fractions to evaluate the integral. Finally, check your answer carefully to avoid any mistakes."
