            "summary": "### Core Concepts\n\nIn this section, we will discuss the core concepts of definite integrals and their properties. A definite integral is a mathematical operation that represents the area under a curve between two points. It is denoted by $$\\\\int_{a}^{b} f(x) dx$$, where $f(x)$ is the function being integrated, and $a$ and $b$ are the limits of integration. The definite integral has several properties that make it a powerful tool in calculus. These properties include:\n\n* The linearity property: $$\\\\int_{a}^{b} [f(x) + g(x)] dx = \\\\int_{a}^{b} f(x) dx + \\\\int_{a}^{b} g(x) dx$$\n* The constant multiple property: $$\\\\int_{a}^{b} cf(x) dx = c \\\\int_{a}^{b} f(x) dx$$\n* The additivity property: $$\\\\int_{a}^{b} f(x) dx = \\\\int_{a}^{c} f(x) dx + \\\\int_{c}^{b} f(x) dx$$\n\nThese properties can be used to simplify and evaluate definite integrals.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\n\nA real-world analogy for definite integrals is the concept of accumulation. Imagine you have a tank that is being filled with water at a rate of $f(x)$ cubic meters per hour. The definite integral of $f(x)$ from $a$ to $b$ represents the total amount of water that has accumulated in the tank between hours $a$ and $b$. This analogy helps to illustrate the concept of definite integrals as a measure of accumulation.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\n\nTo derive the linearity property of definite integrals, we can start with the definition of a definite integral:\n\n$$\\\\int_{a}^{b} f(x) dx = \\\\lim_{n \\\\to \\\\infty} \\\\sum_{i=1}^{n} f(x_i^*) \\\\Delta x$$\n\nwhere $\\\\Delta x = \\\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_i^*$ is a point in the $i^{th}$ subinterval. Using the linearity property of limits, we can rewrite the sum as:\n\n$$\\\\lim_{n \\\\to \\\\infty} \\\\sum_{i=1}^{n} [f(x_i^*) + g(x_i^*)] \\\\Delta x = \\\\lim_{n \\\\to \\\\infty} \\\\sum_{i=1}^{n} f(x_i^*) \\\\Delta x + \\\\lim_{n \\\\to \\\\infty} \\\\sum_{i=1}^{n} g(x_i^*) \\\\Delta x$$\n\nUsing the definition of a definite integral, we can rewrite this as:\n\n$$\\\\int_{a}^{b} [f(x) + g(x)] dx = \\\\int_{a}^{b} f(x) dx + \\\\int_{a}^{b} g(x) dx$$\n\nThis derivation shows that the linearity property of definite integrals is a consequence of the linearity property of limits.\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\n\nWhen evaluating definite integrals on an exam, it is often helpful to use the linearity property to break down the integral into simpler components. For example, if you are given the integral $$\\\\int_{0}^{1} (x^2 + 3x) dx$$, you can use the linearity property to rewrite it as $$\\\\int_{0}^{1} x^2 dx + \\\\int_{0}^{1}"
