            "summary": "### Core Concepts\n\nThe substitution method is a powerful technique for evaluating definite integrals. It involves substituting a new variable into the integral, which simplifies the integration process. This method is based on the chain rule of differentiation, which states that if $f(x)$ is a function of $x$, then the derivative of $f(g(x))$ with respect to $x$ is given by $f'(g(x)) \\cdot g'(x)$.\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dx} f(g(x)) = f'(g(x)) \\cdot g'(x)$$\n\nThe substitution method involves making a substitution of the form $u = g(x)$, which implies $du = g'(x) dx$. This allows us to rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and $du$, which may be easier to integrate.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\n\nImagine you are trying to find the area under a curve that is difficult to integrate directly. You can use the substitution method to simplify the integration process by making a substitution that transforms the curve into a more manageable form. For example, if you are trying to find the area under the curve $y = \\frac{1}{x^2}$, you can make the substitution $u = \\frac{1}{x}$, which implies $du = -\\frac{1}{x^2} dx$. This transforms the integral into $\\int -u^2 du$, which is easy to evaluate.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\n\nTo derive the substitution method, we start with the chain rule of differentiation:\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dx} f(g(x)) = f'(g(x)) \\cdot g'(x)$$\n\nWe can rewrite this as:\n\n$$f'(g(x)) = \\frac{1}{g'(x)} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx} f(g(x))$$\n\nNow, let's make the substitution $u = g(x)$, which implies $du = g'(x) dx$. We can rewrite the integral as:\n\n$$\\int f(g(x)) dx = \\int f(u) \\cdot \\frac{1}{g'(x)} \\cdot g'(x) dx$$\n\n$$= \\int f(u) du$$\n\nThis shows that the substitution method allows us to transform the integral into a simpler form that is easier to evaluate.\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\n\nWhen applying the substitution method, make sure to choose a substitution that simplifies the integration process. Some common substitutions include:\n\n* $u = g(x)$, where $g(x)$ is a function of $x$\n* $u = ax + b$, where $a$ and $b$ are constants\n* $u = \\frac{1}{x}"
