            "summary": "### Core Concepts\n\nIntegration by parts is a technique used to integrate the product of two functions. It is based on the product rule of differentiation, which states that if we have two functions $u$ and $v$, then the derivative of their product is given by:\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dx}(uv) = u\\frac{dv}{dx} + v\\frac{du}{dx}$$\n\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\n$$uv = \\int u\\frac{dv}{dx}dx + \\int v\\frac{du}{dx}dx$$\n\nThis is the fundamental equation of integration by parts. We can use it to integrate any product of two functions.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\n\nImagine you are a librarian, and you have a large collection of books on a shelf. Each book has a certain number of pages, and each page has a certain number of words. If you want to know the total number of words in all the books, you can't just count the words in each book individually. You need to count the words in each page, and then add them up.\n\nSimilarly, when we integrate a product of two functions, we need to break it down into smaller parts and integrate each part separately. This is where integration by parts comes in. We can use it to integrate the product of two functions by breaking it down into smaller parts and integrating each part separately.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\n\nTo derive the formula for integration by parts, we start with the product rule of differentiation:\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dx}(uv) = u\\frac{dv}{dx} + v\\frac{du}{dx}$$\n\nWe can rewrite this as:\n\n$$uv = \\int u\\frac{dv}{dx}dx + \\int v\\frac{du}{dx}dx$$\n\nNow, we can integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x$. This gives us:\n\n$$\\int uv dx = \\int \\left( \\int u\\frac{dv}{dx}dx \\right) dx + \\int \\left( \\int v\\frac{du}{dx}dx \\right) dx$$\n\nUsing the fundamental theorem of calculus, we can simplify the right-hand side of the equation to get:\n\n$$\\int uv dx = uv - \\int v\\frac{du}{dx}dx$$\n\nThis is the formula for integration by parts. We can use it to integrate any product of two functions.\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\n\nWhen using integration by parts, it's essential to choose the right functions for $u$ and $v$. Here are some tips to help you choose the right functions:\n\n* Choose $u$ to be a function that is easy to integrate.\n* Choose $v$ to be a function that is easy to differentiate.\n* Use the formula $uv = \\int u\\frac{dv}{dx}dx + \\int v\\frac{du}{dx}"
