            "summary": "### Core Concepts\n\nCoulomb's Law is a fundamental principle in electrostatics that describes the interaction between two point charges. It states that the force between two point charges is directly proportional to the product of the magnitudes of the charges and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them.\n\n$$F = k \\frac{q_1 q_2}{r^2}$$\n\nwhere $F$ is the electric force, $k$ is Coulomb's constant, $q_1$ and $q_2$ are the magnitudes of the charges, and $r$ is the distance between the charges.\n\nThe electric force can be attractive or repulsive, depending on the signs of the charges. If both charges have the same sign, the force is repulsive, while if they have opposite signs, the force is attractive.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\n\nImagine two magnets, one with a north pole and the other with a south pole. When you bring them close together, they will attract each other. This is similar to how two opposite charges interact. However, if you have two magnets with the same pole facing each other, they will repel each other, similar to how two like charges interact.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\n\nTo derive Coulomb's Law, we can start with the concept of electric potential energy. The electric potential energy between two point charges is given by:\n\n$$U = k \\frac{q_1 q_2}{r}$$\n\nWe can then use the fact that the electric force is the negative derivative of the electric potential energy with respect to distance:\n\n$$F = -\\frac{dU}{dr} = -k \\frac{q_1 q_2}{r^2}"
