            "summary": "### Core Concepts\n\nElectric field and potential are two fundamental concepts in electrostatics. The electric field is a vector field that surrounds charged particles and exerts a force on other charged particles. It is defined as the force per unit charge, and is typically denoted by the symbol $\\mathbf{E}$.\n\nThe electric potential, on the other hand, is a scalar field that represents the potential energy per unit charge at a given point in space. It is denoted by the symbol $V$.\n\nThe electric field and potential are related by the equation $\\mathbf{E} = -\nabla V$, where $\nabla$ is the gradient operator.\n\n$$\\mathbf{E} = -\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial x} \\mathbf{i} - \\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial y} \\mathbf{j} - \\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial z} \\mathbf{k}$$\n\nThe electric potential is also related to the electric field by the equation $V = -\\int \\mathbf{E} \\cdot d\\mathbf{l}$.\n\n$$V = -\\int \\mathbf{E} \\cdot d\\mathbf{l}$$\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\n\nImagine a mountain range with a steep slope. The slope of the mountain represents the electric field, which is a vector field that points from a region of high potential energy to a region of low potential energy. The height of the mountain represents the electric potential, which is a scalar field that represents the potential energy per unit charge at a given point in space.\n\nAs you climb up the mountain, you are moving against the electric field, which means you are moving against the force that is exerted on you by the charged particles. The steeper the slope, the stronger the electric field, and the more force you will experience.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\n\nTo derive the relationship between the electric field and potential, we start with the definition of the electric field as the force per unit charge.\n\n$$\\mathbf{E} = \\frac{\\mathbf{F}}{q}$$\n\nWe can then use the gradient operator to express the force in terms of the potential energy.\n\n$$\\mathbf{F} = -\nabla U$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation for the electric field, we get:\n\n$$\\mathbf{E} = -\\frac{\nabla U}{q}$$\n\nSince the potential energy is related to the electric potential by the equation $U = qV$, we can substitute this into the equation for the electric field to get:\n\n$$\\mathbf{E} = -\\frac{\nabla (qV)}{q}$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get:\n\n$$\\mathbf{E} = -\nabla V$$\n\nThis is the relationship between the electric field and potential that we derived earlier.\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\n\nWhen solving problems involving electric fields and potentials, make sure to use the correct units. The electric field is typically measured in units of newtons per coulomb (N/C), while the electric potential is typically measured in units of volts (V).\n\nWhen evaluating the electric field at a given point, make sure to consider the contributions from all the charged particles in the system. This may involve integrating the electric field over a region of space.\n\nWhen evaluating the electric potential at a given point, make sure to consider the contributions from all the charged particles in the system. This may involve integrating the electric field over a region of space.\n\nWhen solving problems involving electric fields and potentials, make sure to use the correct signs. The electric field points from a region of high potential energy to a region of low potential energy, so it points in the opposite direction of the gradient of the potential energy.\n\n$$\\mathbf{E} = -\nabla V$$\n\nThis means that if the potential energy is increasing in a particular direction, the electric field will point in the opposite direction.\n\nWhen solving problems involving electric fields and potentials, make sure to use the correct equations. The electric field is related to the potential energy by the equation $\\mathbf{E} = -\nabla V$, while the electric potential is related to the electric field by the equation $V = -\\int \\mathbf{E} \\cdot d\\mathbf{l}$."
