            "summary": "### Core Concepts\n\nElectric potential, also known as voltage, is the potential difference per unit charge between two points in an electric field. It is a scalar quantity and is measured in volts (V). The electric potential at a point in space is defined as the work done per unit charge in moving a test charge from infinity to that point against the electric field.\n\n$$V = \\frac{W}{q}$$\n\nwhere $V$ is the electric potential, $W$ is the work done, and $q$ is the magnitude of the test charge.\n\nThe electric potential due to a point charge is given by:\n\n$$V = \\frac{k \\cdot q}{r}$$\n\nwhere $k$ is Coulomb's constant, $q$ is the magnitude of the point charge, and $r$ is the distance from the point charge.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\n\nImagine a hill with a gentle slope. The height of the hill at any point represents the electric potential at that point. A ball rolled from the top of the hill to the bottom will gain kinetic energy, just like a charge moving from a region of high electric potential to a region of low electric potential will gain kinetic energy.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\n\nTo derive the equation for electric potential due to a point charge, we start with the definition of electric potential:\n\n$$V = \\frac{W}{q}$$\n\nWe can rewrite the work done as:\n\n$$W = -\\int_{\\infty}^{r} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot d\\mathbf{l}$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}$ is the electric force on the test charge and $d\\mathbf{l}$ is the displacement of the test charge.\n\nFor a point charge, the electric force is given by:\n\n$$\\mathbf{F} = \\frac{k \\cdot q}{r^2} \\mathbf{\\hat{r}}$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{\\hat{r}}$ is the unit vector pointing from the point charge to the test charge.\n\nSubstituting this into the expression for work done, we get:\n\n$$W = -\\int_{\\infty}^{r} \\frac{k \\cdot q}{r^2} \\mathbf{\\hat{r}} \\cdot d\\mathbf{l}$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we get:\n\n$$W = -\\frac{k \\cdot q}{r}$$\n\nSubstituting this back into the definition of electric potential, we get:\n\n$$V = \\frac{k \\cdot q}{r}$$\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\n\nWhen solving problems involving electric potential, make sure to identify the point charge and the distance from the point charge to the point of interest. Use the equation:\n\n$$V = \\frac{k \\cdot q}{r}"
