            "summary": "### Core Concepts\n\nElectric potential and potential difference are fundamental concepts in electrostatics. Electric potential is defined as the potential energy per unit charge at a given point in an electric field. It is a scalar quantity and is measured in volts (V). The electric potential at a point is denoted by $V$ and is given by the equation:\n\n$$V = \\frac{W}{q}$$\n\nwhere $W$ is the work done in moving a charge $q$ from infinity to the point.\n\nThe potential difference between two points is the difference in electric potential between the two points. It is a measure of the work done in moving a charge from one point to the other. The potential difference between two points is given by the equation:\n\n$$\\Delta V = V_2 - V_1$$\n\nwhere $V_2$ and $V_1$ are the electric potentials at the two points.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\n\nImagine a hiker climbing a mountain. The hiker starts at sea level and climbs to the top of the mountain. The height of the mountain represents the electric potential. The hiker has to do work to climb the mountain, and the work done is proportional to the height of the mountain. Similarly, in an electric field, a charge has to do work to move from one point to another, and the work done is proportional to the electric potential difference between the two points.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\n\nThe electric potential at a point in an electric field is given by the equation:\n\n$$V = \\frac{W}{q} = \\frac{k \\cdot q}{r}$$\n\nwhere $k$ is Coulomb's constant, $q$ is the charge, and $r$ is the distance from the charge to the point.\n\nThe potential difference between two points is given by the equation:\n\n$$\\Delta V = V_2 - V_1 = \\frac{k \\cdot q_2}{r_2} - \\frac{k \\cdot q_1}{r_1}$$\n\nwhere $q_2$ and $q_1$ are the charges at the two points, and $r_2$ and $r_1$ are the distances from the charges to the points.\n\nThe electric potential at a point due to a collection of charges is given by the equation:\n\n$$V = \\frac{k \\cdot q_1}{r_1} + \\frac{k \\cdot q_2}{r_2} + \\cdots$$\n\nwhere $q_1$, $q_2$, $\\cdots$ are the charges, and $r_1$, $r_2$, $\\cdots$ are the distances from the charges to the point.\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\n\n* The electric potential at a point is a scalar quantity, so it has no direction.\n* The potential difference between two points is a measure of the work done in moving a charge from one point to the other.\n* The electric potential at a point due to a collection of charges is the sum of the electric potentials due to each charge.\n* In a uniform electric field, the electric potential is constant, and the potential difference between two points is proportional to the distance between the points.\n* In a non-uniform electric field, the electric potential varies with distance, and the potential difference between two points is not proportional to the distance between the points.\n* To solve problems involving electric potential and potential difference, use the equations:\n\n$$V = \\frac{k \\cdot q}{r}$$\n\n$$\\Delta V = V_2 - V_1 = \\frac{k \\cdot q_2}{r_2} - \\frac{k \\cdot q_1}{r_1}$$\n\n$$V = \\frac{k \\cdot q_1}{r_1} + \\frac{k \\cdot q_2}{r_2}"
