            "summary": "### Core Concepts\nElectric potential, also known as voltage, is the potential difference per unit charge between two points in an electric field. It is a scalar quantity and is measured in volts (V). The electric potential at a point is defined as the work done per unit charge in moving a test charge from infinity to that point against the electric field.\n\nThe electric potential at a point in a uniform electric field is given by the equation:\n\n$$V = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\frac{q}{r}$$\n\nwhere $V$ is the electric potential, $q$ is the charge, $r$ is the distance from the charge, and $\\epsilon_0$ is the electric constant (also known as the permittivity of free space).\n\nEquipotential surfaces are surfaces where the electric potential is constant. These surfaces are perpendicular to the electric field lines and are used to define the electric potential at a point.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\nImagine a mountain range with peaks and valleys. The height of the mountain range at any point represents the electric potential at that point. The equipotential surfaces would be the horizontal planes that are perpendicular to the mountain range. Just as you can move from one point to another on the mountain range by climbing or descending, you can move from one equipotential surface to another by moving through the electric field.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\nThe electric potential at a point in a uniform electric field can be derived using the following steps:\n\n1. Consider a test charge $q$ at a distance $r$ from a point charge $Q$.\n2. The work done in moving the test charge from infinity to a distance $r$ against the electric field is given by:\n\n$$W = \\int_{\\infty}^{r} F \\, dr$$\n\nwhere $F$ is the force on the test charge due to the electric field.\n3. The force on the test charge due to the electric field is given by:\n\n$$F = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\frac{Qq}{r^2}$$\n\n4. Substituting this expression for $F$ into the equation for $W$, we get:\n\n$$W = \\int_{\\infty}^{r} \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\frac{Qq}{r^2} \\, dr$$\n\n5. Evaluating this integral, we get:\n\n$$W = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\frac{Qq}{r}$$\n\n6. The electric potential at a point is defined as the work done per unit charge in moving a test charge from infinity to that point against the electric field. Therefore, the electric potential at a point is given by:\n\n$$V = \\frac{W}{q} = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\frac{Q}{r}$$\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\nWhen solving problems involving electric potential, make sure to use the correct equation for the electric potential at a point in a uniform electric field. Also, be careful when using the concept of equipotential surfaces, as they are perpendicular to the electric field lines.\n\nIn exam questions, you may be asked to find the electric potential at a point due to a given charge distribution. Use the equation for the electric potential at a point in a uniform electric field to solve these problems.\n\nWhen solving problems involving electric potential, make sure to use the correct units for the electric potential, which is volts (V). Also, be careful when using the concept of work done in moving a test charge from infinity to a point against the electric field, as it is used to define the electric potential at a point."
