            "id": "electrostatics_3_3",
            "title": "Energy Stored in a Capacitor",
            "topic": "Capacitance and Capacitors",
            "duration": 30,
            "summary": "### Core Concepts\n\nThe energy stored in a capacitor is a fundamental concept in electrostatics, and it is defined as the work done in charging the capacitor. The energy stored in a capacitor can be calculated using the formula:\n\n$$W = \\frac{1}{2}QV$$\n\nwhere $W$ is the energy stored, $Q$ is the charge on the capacitor, and $V$ is the potential difference across the capacitor.\n\nThe energy stored in a capacitor can also be expressed in terms of the capacitance $C$ and the potential difference $V$ as:\n\n$$W = \\frac{1}{2}CV^2$$\n\nThis equation shows that the energy stored in a capacitor is directly proportional to the capacitance and the square of the potential difference.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\n\nThe energy stored in a capacitor can be thought of as the energy stored in a compressed spring. When a spring is compressed, it stores potential energy, which is released when the spring is allowed to expand. Similarly, when a capacitor is charged, it stores energy, which is released when the capacitor is discharged.\n\nImagine a rubber band that is stretched to its maximum extent. The energy stored in the rubber band is proportional to the square of the distance it is stretched. Similarly, the energy stored in a capacitor is proportional to the square of the potential difference across it.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\n\nThe energy stored in a capacitor can be derived from the work done in charging the capacitor. Let's consider a capacitor with capacitance $C$ and a potential difference $V$ across it. The charge on the capacitor is given by:\n\n$$Q = CV$$\n\nThe work done in charging the capacitor is given by:\n\n$$W = \\int_0^Q V \\, dQ$$\n\nSubstituting $V = Q/C$ into the integral, we get:\n\n$$W = \\int_0^Q \\frac{Q}{C} \\, dQ$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we get:\n\n$$W = \\frac{1}{2} \\frac{Q^2}{C}$$\n\nSubstituting $Q = CV$, we get:\n\n$$W = \\frac{1}{2} CV^2$$\n\nThis equation shows that the energy stored in a capacitor is directly proportional to the capacitance and the square of the potential difference.\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\n\nWhen solving problems involving the energy stored in a capacitor, make sure to use the correct formula:\n\n$$W = \\frac{1}{2}CV^2$$\n\nDon't forget to use the correct units for the variables. The energy stored in a capacitor is typically measured in joules (J).\n\nCommon exam pitfalls to watch out for:\n\n* Using the wrong formula for the energy stored in a capacitor.\n* Forgetting to use the correct units for the variables.\n* Not considering the direction of the potential difference across the capacitor.\n\nTo solve problems involving the energy stored in a capacitor, follow these steps:\n\n1. Identify the capacitance and the potential difference across the capacitor.\n2. Use the correct formula to calculate the energy stored in the capacitor.\n3. Make sure to use the correct units for the variables.\n4. Check your answer to make sure it makes physical sense."
