            "summary": "### Core Concepts\n\nDielectrics are insulating materials that can be polarized by an external electric field. When an electric field is applied to a dielectric material, the electrons in the material are displaced from their original positions, resulting in a separation of positive and negative charges. This separation of charges creates an electric field within the material, which opposes the external electric field.\n\nThe polarization of a dielectric material can be described by the following equation:\n\n$$\\mathbf{P} = \\epsilon_0 \\chi_e \\mathbf{E}$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{P}$ is the polarization density, $\\epsilon_0$ is the electric constant (also known as the permittivity of free space), $\\chi_e$ is the electric susceptibility of the material, and $\\mathbf{E}$ is the external electric field.\n\nThe electric displacement field $\\mathbf{D}$ is related to the electric field $\\mathbf{E}$ and the polarization density $\\mathbf{P}$ by the following equation:\n\n$$\\mathbf{D} = \\epsilon_0 \\mathbf{E} + \\mathbf{P}$$\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\n\nImagine a dielectric material as a collection of tiny dipoles, each consisting of a positive charge and a negative charge separated by a small distance. When an external electric field is applied to the material, the dipoles align themselves with the electric field, resulting in a separation of positive and negative charges. This separation of charges creates an electric field within the material, which opposes the external electric field.\n\nFor example, consider a dielectric material such as glass or plastic. When an electric field is applied to this material, the electrons in the material are displaced from their original positions, resulting in a separation of positive and negative charges. This separation of charges creates an electric field within the material, which opposes the external electric field.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\n\nTo derive the equation for the polarization density $\\mathbf{P}$, we start with the following equation:\n\n$$\\mathbf{F} = q \\mathbf{E}$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}$ is the force experienced by a charge $q$ in an electric field $\\mathbf{E}$.\n\nWe can rewrite this equation in terms of the dipole moment $\\mathbf{p}$ as follows:\n\n$$\\mathbf{F} = \\frac{\\partial U}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} = \\frac{\\partial (U_0 + \\mathbf{p} \\cdot \\mathbf{E})}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} = \\mathbf{p} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{E}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} + \\mathbf{E} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{p}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}}$$\n\nwhere $U_0$ is the potential energy of the dipole in the absence of an electric field.\n\nUsing the definition of the dipole moment $\\mathbf{p}$, we can rewrite the second term on the right-hand side of this equation as follows:\n\n$$\\mathbf{E} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{p}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} = \\mathbf{E} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial (q \\mathbf{r})}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} = q \\mathbf{E}$$\n\nSubstituting this expression into the previous equation, we get:\n\n$$\\mathbf{F} = \\mathbf{p} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{E}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} + q \\mathbf{E}$$\n\nWe can now use this equation to derive the equation for the polarization density $\\mathbf{P}$.\n\nUsing the definition of the polarization density $\\mathbf{P}$, we can rewrite the equation for the force $\\mathbf{F}$ as follows:\n\n$$\\mathbf{F} = \\mathbf{P} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{E}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} + q \\mathbf{E}$$\n\nWe can now use this equation to derive the equation for the polarization density $\\mathbf{P}$.\n\nTaking the derivative of this equation with respect to $\\mathbf{r}$, we get:\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{F}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} = \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{P}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{E}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} + \\mathbf{P} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial^2 \\mathbf{E}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}^2} + q \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{E}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}}$$\n\nUsing the definition of the electric field $\\mathbf{E}$, we can rewrite the first term on the right-hand side of this equation as follows:\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{P}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{E}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} = \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{P}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} \\cdot \\frac{q}{\\epsilon_0}$$\n\nSubstituting this expression into the previous equation, we get:\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{F}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} = \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{P}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} \\cdot \\frac{q}{\\epsilon_0} + \\mathbf{P} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial^2 \\mathbf{E}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}^2} + q \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{E}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}}$$\n\nWe can now use this equation to derive the equation for the polarization density $\\mathbf{P}$.\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get:\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{P}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} = \\frac{q}{\\epsilon_0} \\mathbf{P} + \\frac{q}{\\epsilon_0} \\mathbf{E}$$\n\nUsing the definition of the electric susceptibility $\\chi_e$, we can rewrite this equation as follows:\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{P}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} = \\frac{q}{\\epsilon_0} (\\chi_e \\mathbf{E} + \\mathbf{E})$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get:\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{P}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} = \\frac{q}{\\epsilon_0} \\chi_e \\mathbf{E} + \\frac{q}{\\epsilon_0} \\mathbf{E}$$\n\nUsing the definition of the polarization density $\\mathbf{P}$, we can rewrite this equation as follows:\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{P}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} = \\frac{q}{\\epsilon_0} \\chi_e \\mathbf{E} + \\frac{q}{\\epsilon_0} \\frac{\\mathbf{P}}{\\epsilon_0}$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get:\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{P}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} = \\frac{q}{\\epsilon_0^2} \\chi_e \\mathbf{E} + \\frac{q}{\\epsilon_0^2} \\mathbf{P}$$\n\nUsing the definition of the electric constant $\\epsilon_0$, we can rewrite this equation as follows:\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{P}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} = \\frac{q}{\\epsilon_0} \\chi_e \\mathbf{E} + \\frac{q}{\\epsilon_0} \\mathbf{P}$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get:\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{P}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} = \\frac{q}{\\epsilon_0} (\\chi_e \\mathbf{E} + \\mathbf{P})$$\n\nUsing the definition of the electric displacement field $\\mathbf{D}$, we can rewrite this equation as follows:\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{D}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} = \\frac{q}{\\epsilon_0} (\\chi_e \\mathbf{E} + \\mathbf{P})$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get:\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{D}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} = \\frac{q}{\\epsilon_0} \\mathbf{E} + \\frac{q}{\\epsilon_0} \\mathbf{P}$$\n\nUsing the definition of the electric field $\\mathbf{E}$, we can rewrite this equation as follows:\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{D}}{\\partial \\mathbf{r}} = \\frac{q}{\\epsilon_0} \\mathbf{E} + \\frac{q}{\\epsilon_0} \\frac{\\mathbf{P}}{\\epsilon_0}"
