            "summary": "### Core Concepts\n\nPolarization is the process by which a dielectric material becomes electrically polarized in the presence of an external electric field. This occurs when the electric field causes the electrons in the material to shift slightly, resulting in a separation of positive and negative charges within the material.\n\nThe electric displacement field, denoted by $$\\mathbf{D}$$, is a measure of the electric field within a dielectric material. It is related to the electric field $$\\mathbf{E}$$ by the equation $$\\mathbf{D} = \\epsilon \\mathbf{E}$$, where $$\\epsilon$$ is the permittivity of the material.\n\nThe polarization $$\\mathbf{P}$$ of a dielectric material is defined as the dipole moment per unit volume. It is related to the electric field by the equation $$\\mathbf{P} = \\epsilon_0 (\\chi_e - 1) \\mathbf{E}$$, where $$\\epsilon_0$$ is the vacuum permittivity and $$\\chi_e$$ is the electric susceptibility of the material.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\n\nImagine a dielectric material as a collection of tiny dipoles, each consisting of a positive charge and a negative charge separated by a small distance. When an external electric field is applied, the dipoles align themselves with the field, resulting in a net polarization of the material. This is similar to how a compass needle aligns itself with the Earth's magnetic field.\n\nConsider a dielectric material with a high electric susceptibility, such as a ferroelectric material. When an external electric field is applied, the material becomes highly polarized, resulting in a large electric displacement field. This can lead to interesting phenomena, such as the formation of domains and the creation of electric dipoles.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\n\nTo derive the equation for the electric displacement field, we start with the definition of the electric field $$\\mathbf{E}$$ as the force per unit charge. We can then use the Lorentz force equation to relate the electric field to the electric displacement field:\n\n$$\\mathbf{F} = q (\\mathbf{E} + \\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{B})$$\n\nwhere $$\\mathbf{F}$$ is the force on a charge $$q$$, $$\\mathbf{v}$$ is the velocity of the charge, and $$\\mathbf{B}$$ is the magnetic field.\n\nAssuming that the magnetic field is zero, we can simplify the equation to:\n\n$$\\mathbf{F} = q \\mathbf{E}$$\n\nWe can then use the definition of the electric displacement field $$\\mathbf{D}$$ to relate it to the electric field:\n\n$$\\mathbf{D} = \\epsilon \\mathbf{E}$$\n\nwhere $$\\epsilon$$ is the permittivity of the material.\n\nTo derive the equation for the polarization $$\\mathbf{P}$$, we start with the definition of the dipole moment $$\\mathbf{p}$$ as the product of the charge $$q$$ and the distance $$d$$ between the positive and negative charges:\n\n$$\\mathbf{p} = q d$$\n\nWe can then use the definition of the polarization $$\\mathbf{P}$$ as the dipole moment per unit volume:\n\n$$\\mathbf{P} = \\frac{\\mathbf{p}}{V}$$\n\nwhere $$V$$ is the volume of the material.\n\nAssuming that the material is homogeneous and isotropic, we can simplify the equation to:\n\n$$\\mathbf{P} = \\epsilon_0 (\\chi_e - 1) \\mathbf{E}$$\n\nwhere $$\\epsilon_0$$ is the vacuum permittivity and $$\\chi_e$$ is the electric susceptibility of the material.\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\n\nWhen solving problems involving dielectrics and electric fields, it's essential to remember the following key points:\n\n* The electric displacement field $$\\mathbf{D}$$ is a measure of the electric field within a dielectric material.\n* The polarization $$\\mathbf{P}"
