            "id": "electrostatics_4_3",
            "title": "Electric Displacement and Gauss's Law",
            "topic": "Dielectrics and Electric Field in Matter",
            "duration": 30,
            "summary": "### Core Concepts\n\nElectric displacement, also known as electric flux density, is a measure of the amount of electric field that passes through a given surface. It is defined as the dot product of the electric field and the area vector of the surface. Mathematically, it can be represented as:\n\n$$\\mathbf{D} = \\epsilon_0 \\mathbf{E}$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{D}$ is the electric displacement, $\\epsilon_0$ is the electric constant (also known as the permittivity of free space), and $\\mathbf{E}$ is the electric field.\n\nGauss's law for electric displacement is a fundamental concept in electrostatics that relates the electric displacement to the charge enclosed within a given volume. It states that the total electric displacement through a closed surface is proportional to the charge enclosed within that surface. Mathematically, it can be represented as:\n\n$$\\oint_S \\mathbf{D} \\cdot d\\mathbf{A} = Q_{enc}$$\n\nwhere $S$ is the closed surface, $\\mathbf{D}$ is the electric displacement, $d\\mathbf{A}$ is the area vector of the surface, and $Q_{enc}$ is the charge enclosed within the surface.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\n\nImagine a garden hose with a spray nozzle. The nozzle can be thought of as a surface that the water (electric field) is passing through. The amount of water that passes through the nozzle (electric displacement) depends on the pressure of the water (electric field) and the size of the nozzle (area of the surface). If the pressure of the water is high and the nozzle is small, more water will pass through the nozzle, just like a strong electric field passing through a small surface will result in a large electric displacement.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\n\nTo derive Gauss's law for electric displacement, we can start by considering a small volume element within a larger volume. The electric displacement through the surface of the small volume element can be represented as:\n\n$$\\mathbf{D} \\cdot d\\mathbf{A} = \\epsilon_0 \\mathbf{E} \\cdot d\\mathbf{A}$$\n\nUsing the definition of the electric field, we can rewrite this as:\n\n$$\\mathbf{D} \\cdot d\\mathbf{A} = \\epsilon_0 \\frac{Q}{\\epsilon_0 V} d\\mathbf{A}$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is the charge within the small volume element and $V$ is the volume of the small volume element.\n\nIntegrating this expression over the entire volume, we get:\n\n$$\\oint_S \\mathbf{D} \\cdot d\\mathbf{A} = \\epsilon_0 \\frac{Q_{enc}}{\\epsilon_0 V} \\oint_S d\\mathbf{A}$$\n\nSimplifying this expression, we get:\n\n$$\\oint_S \\mathbf{D} \\cdot d\\mathbf{A} = Q_{enc}$$\n\nwhich is Gauss's law for electric displacement.\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\n\nWhen applying Gauss's law for electric displacement, make sure to identify the charge enclosed within the surface and the electric displacement through the surface. Use the formula:\n\n$$\\oint_S \\mathbf{D} \\cdot d\\mathbf{A} = Q_{enc}"
