            "summary": "### Core Concepts\nUnits are fundamental quantities used to express physical quantities. They are the building blocks of measurement and are used to describe the magnitude of a quantity. There are two types of units: fundamental units and derived units. Fundamental units are the basic units of measurement, such as length, mass, and time. Derived units are units that are derived from fundamental units, such as velocity and acceleration.\n\nDimensions are the powers to which the fundamental units are raised to express a physical quantity. For example, the dimension of velocity is length/time. Dimensions are used to classify physical quantities into different categories. The number of dimensions of a physical quantity is equal to the number of fundamental units required to express it.\n\nErrors are uncertainties in measurement. They can be due to various factors such as instrument calibration, human error, and environmental conditions. Errors can be classified into two types: systematic errors and random errors. Systematic errors are consistent errors that occur due to a specific cause, while random errors are unpredictable errors that occur due to various factors.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\nImagine you are on a road trip and you want to measure the distance between two cities. You can use a ruler to measure the distance, but the ruler has a certain error associated with it. If you use the ruler to measure the distance, you will get a value that is close to the actual distance, but not exactly the same. This is because the ruler has a certain error associated with it.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\n$$\n\\text{Dimension of a physical quantity} = \\text{powers of fundamental units}\n$$\n\nFor example, the dimension of velocity is length/time, which can be expressed as:\n\n$$\n\\text{dimension of velocity} = \\text{length}^1 \\times \\text{time}^{-1}\n$$\n\nThe dimension of acceleration is velocity/time, which can be expressed as:\n\n$$\n\\text{dimension of acceleration} = \\text{length}^1 \\times \\text{time}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThe error in measurement can be expressed as:\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\sigma_x \\sqrt{n}"
