            "duration": 30,
            "summary": "### Core Concepts\n\nVectors are mathematical objects that have both magnitude and direction. They can be represented graphically as arrows in a coordinate system, with the length of the arrow representing the magnitude and the direction of the arrow representing the direction of the vector. Vectors can be denoted by boldface letters, such as $\\mathbf{a}$, or by arrows above letters, such as $\\overrightarrow{a}$. The magnitude of a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is denoted by $|\\mathbf{a}|$ or $a$, and the direction of a vector is denoted by the unit vector $\\hat{\\mathbf{a}}$.\n\nThe sum of two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is denoted by $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}$ and is defined as the vector that results from adding the corresponding components of the two vectors. The difference of two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is denoted by $\\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{b}$ and is defined as the vector that results from subtracting the corresponding components of the two vectors.\n\nThe dot product of two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is denoted by $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}$ and is defined as the product of the magnitudes of the two vectors and the cosine of the angle between them: $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = |\\mathbf{a}| |\\mathbf{b}| \\cos \\theta$. The cross product of two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is denoted by $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}$ and is defined as the vector that results from the right-hand rule: $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = |\\mathbf{a}| |\\mathbf{b}| \\sin \\theta \\hat{\\mathbf{n}}$, where $\\hat{\\mathbf{n}}$ is the unit vector perpendicular to both $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\n\nImagine you are on a hike and you need to get to a campsite that is 5 miles away in the north direction. You can represent the direction of the campsite as a vector $\\mathbf{a}$, and the direction of your current location as a vector $\\mathbf{b}$. The sum of the two vectors $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}$ would represent the direction of the campsite relative to your current location. If you need to get to the campsite, you would need to follow the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}$.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\n\nThe dot product of two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ can be derived using the following equation:\n\n$$\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = |\\mathbf{a}| |\\mathbf{b}| \\cos \\theta$$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle between the two vectors. This equation can be derived by considering the projection of one vector onto the other. The projection of vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is given by:\n\n$$\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}} \\mathbf{a} = \\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{|\\mathbf{b}|^2} \\mathbf{b}$$\n\nThe dot product of two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ can be used to find the angle between them. The angle $\\theta$ between two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is given by:\n\n$$\\cos \\theta = \\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{|\\mathbf{a}| |\\mathbf{b}|}$$\n\nThe cross product of two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ can be derived using the following equation:\n\n$$\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = |\\mathbf{a}| |\\mathbf{b}| \\sin \\theta \\hat{\\mathbf{n}}$$\n\nwhere $\\hat{\\mathbf{n}}$ is the unit vector perpendicular to both $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$. This equation can be derived by considering the area of the parallelogram formed by the two vectors.\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\n\nWhen taking the exam, make sure to use the correct notation for vectors. Use boldface letters or arrows above letters to denote vectors. Make sure to use the correct units for the magnitude of the vector. The magnitude of a vector is typically denoted by the same unit as the coordinate system, such as meters or feet.\n\nWhen adding or subtracting vectors, make sure to use the correct components. The sum of two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is denoted by $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}$ and is defined as the vector that results from adding the corresponding components of the two vectors.\n\nWhen taking the dot product or cross product of two vectors, make sure to use the correct formula. The dot product of two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is denoted by $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}$ and is defined as the product of the magnitudes of the two vectors and the cosine of the angle between them. The cross product of two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is denoted by $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}$ and is defined as the vector that results from the right-hand rule."
