            "summary": "### Core Concepts\nIn vector algebra, vector addition and subtraction are fundamental operations that allow us to combine and manipulate vectors in various ways. A vector is a mathematical object that has both magnitude and direction. When we add or subtract vectors, we are essentially combining their magnitudes and directions to obtain a new vector.\n\nThere are two main ways to add vectors: graphically and algebraically. Graphically, we can add vectors by drawing them head-to-tail and then drawing an arrow from the tail of the first vector to the head of the last vector. Algebraically, we can add vectors by using the parallelogram law, which states that the sum of two vectors is equal to the diagonal of the parallelogram formed by the two vectors.\n\nThe parallelogram law can be expressed mathematically as:\n\n$$\\mathbf{A} + \\mathbf{B} = \\mathbf{C}$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{B}$ are the two vectors being added, and $\\mathbf{C}$ is the resulting vector.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\nImagine you are on a hike and you need to get to a destination that is 5 miles east and 3 miles north of your current location. You can either walk 5 miles east and then 3 miles north, or you can walk 3 miles north and then 5 miles east. In both cases, you will end up at the same destination, but you will have taken a different path. This is similar to adding vectors, where we are combining the magnitudes and directions of two vectors to obtain a new vector.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\nTo derive the parallelogram law, we can start by drawing a parallelogram with two adjacent sides represented by the vectors $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{B}$. The diagonal of the parallelogram represents the sum of the two vectors, which we can call $\\mathbf{C}$.\n\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem, we can express the magnitude of the diagonal as:\n\n$$|\\mathbf{C}| = \\sqrt{|\\mathbf{A}|^2 + |\\mathbf{B}|^2 + 2|\\mathbf{A}||\\mathbf{B}|\\cos\\theta}$$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle between the two vectors.\n\nWe can also express the direction of the diagonal as:\n\n$$\\theta_C = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{|\\mathbf{B}|\\sin\\theta}{|\\mathbf{A}| + |\\mathbf{B}|\\cos\\theta}\\right)$$\n\nwhere $\\theta_C$ is the angle between the diagonal and the x-axis.\n\nBy combining these two equations, we can express the sum of the two vectors as:\n\n$$\\mathbf{C} = |\\mathbf{C}|\\cos\\theta_C\\mathbf{i} + |\\mathbf{C}|\\sin\\theta_C\\mathbf{j}$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ are the unit vectors in the x and y directions, respectively.\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\nWhen adding vectors, it's essential to remember that the order of the vectors does not matter. This means that $\\mathbf{A} + \\mathbf{B} = \\mathbf{B} + \\mathbf{A}$.\n\nIn exams, you may be asked to add vectors graphically or algebraically. To add vectors graphically, use the parallelogram law and draw the vectors head-to-tail. To add vectors algebraically, use the formula:\n\n$$\\mathbf{C} = |\\mathbf{C}|\\cos\\theta_C\\mathbf{i} + |\\mathbf{C}|\\sin\\theta_C\\mathbf{j}$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{C}$ is the sum of the two vectors, $|\\mathbf{C}|$ is the magnitude of the sum, $\\theta_C$ is the angle between the sum and the x-axis, and $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ are the unit vectors in the x and y directions, respectively.\n\nWhen subtracting vectors, use the formula:\n\n$$\\mathbf{A} - \\mathbf{B} = \\mathbf{A} + (-\\mathbf{B})$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{B}$ are the two vectors being subtracted, and $-\\mathbf{B}$ is the negative of vector $\\mathbf{B}$."
