            "summary": "### Core Concepts\nProjectile motion is a type of motion where an object moves under the influence of gravity, and its trajectory is parabolic in shape. The key concept in projectile motion is the range, which is the horizontal distance traveled by the object.\n\nThe equations of motion for an object under projectile motion are given by:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= v_0 \\cos \\theta t \\\ny &= v_0 \\sin \\theta t - \\frac{1}{2} g t^2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere $v_0$ is the initial velocity, $\\theta$ is the angle of projection, $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity, and $t$ is time.\n\nThe time of flight is given by:\n$$\nt = \\frac{2 v_0 \\sin \\theta}{g}\n$$\nand the maximum height is given by:\n$$\nh = \\frac{v_0^2 \\sin^2 \\theta}{2g}\n$$\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\nImagine throwing a ball straight up in the air. As it reaches its maximum height, it momentarily stops and then starts falling back down. This is an example of projectile motion, where the ball is under the influence of gravity.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\nTo derive the equations of motion, we start with the equations of motion for an object under constant acceleration:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= x_0 + v_{0x} t \\\ny &= y_0 + v_{0y} t - \\frac{1}{2} g t^2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are the initial positions, $v_{0x}$ and $v_{0y}$ are the initial velocities, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nSince the object is projected at an angle $\\theta$, we can write:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{0x} &= v_0 \\cos \\theta \\\nv_{0y} &= v_0 \\sin \\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSubstituting these values into the equations of motion, we get:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= x_0 + v_0 \\cos \\theta t \\\ny &= y_0 + v_0 \\sin \\theta t - \\frac{1}{2} g t^2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince the object is projected from the origin, we can set $x_0 = 0$ and $y_0 = 0$. Therefore, the equations of motion become:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= v_0 \\cos \\theta t \\\ny &= v_0 \\sin \\theta t - \\frac{1}{2} g t^2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\nTo solve problems on projectile motion, you need to use the equations of motion and the range formula. The range formula is given by:\n$$\nR = \\frac{v_0^2 \\sin 2 \\theta}{g}"
