            "summary": "### Core Concepts\nRelative motion refers to the motion of an object with respect to another object. It is a fundamental concept in physics that helps us understand the motion of objects in various situations. The concept of relative motion is based on the idea that motion is relative and not absolute.\n\nThe key equation for relative motion is the equation of relative velocity, which is given by:\n\n$$\\vec{v}_{AB} = \\vec{v}_A - \\vec{v}_B$$\n\nwhere $\\vec{v}_{AB}$ is the relative velocity of object $A$ with respect to object $B$, $\\vec{v}_A$ is the velocity of object $A$, and $\\vec{v}_B$ is the velocity of object $B$.\n\nAnother important concept related to relative motion is the concept of reference frames. A reference frame is a coordinate system with respect to which the motion of an object is described. The choice of reference frame can affect the description of motion and the calculation of relative velocities.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\nImagine you are on a train traveling at a speed of 60 km/h. You throw a ball straight up in the air. From your perspective on the train, the ball goes straight up and comes straight down. However, from the perspective of a person standing on the platform watching the train pass by, the ball appears to be moving in a curved path due to the motion of the train. This illustrates the concept of relative motion, where the motion of an object appears different depending on the reference frame.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\nTo derive the equation of relative velocity, we can use the concept of velocity addition. Let's consider two objects $A$ and $B$ moving with velocities $\\vec{v}_A$ and $\\vec{v}_B$ respectively. We want to find the velocity of object $A$ with respect to object $B$. We can use the following steps:\n\n1. Resolve the velocities $\\vec{v}_A$ and $\\vec{v}_B$ into their components along the $x$ and $y$ axes.\n2. Add the components of the velocities along the $x$ and $y$ axes to get the components of the relative velocity $\\vec{v}_{AB}$.\n3. Use the Pythagorean theorem to find the magnitude of the relative velocity $\\vec{v}_{AB}$.\n\nThe derivation is as follows:\n\n$$\\vec{v}_A = v_{Ax} \\hat{i} + v_{Ay} \\hat{j}$$\n$$\\vec{v}_B = v_{Bx} \\hat{i} + v_{By} \\hat{j}$$\n\n$$\\vec{v}_{AB} = \\vec{v}_A - \\vec{v}_B$$\n$$= (v_{Ax} - v_{Bx}) \\hat{i} + (v_{Ay} - v_{By}) \\hat{j}$$\n\n$$\\left|\\vec{v}_{AB}\\right| = \\sqrt{(v_{Ax} - v_{Bx})^2 + (v_{Ay} - v_{By})^2}$$\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\nWhen solving problems involving relative motion, make sure to identify the reference frame and the velocities of the objects involved. Use the equation of relative velocity to find the relative velocity of one object with respect to another.\n\nCommon exam pitfalls to avoid:\n\n* Failing to identify the reference frame or the velocities of the objects involved.\n* Not using the equation of relative velocity to find the relative velocity of one object with respect to another.\n* Not considering the direction of the relative velocity.\n\nTips for problem-solving:\n\n* Use the concept of velocity addition to derive the equation of relative velocity.\n* Resolve the velocities into their components along the $x$ and $y$ axes.\n* Use the Pythagorean theorem to find the magnitude of the relative velocity.\n* Make sure to identify the reference frame and the velocities of the objects involved."
