            "summary": "### Core Concepts\n\nVector functions are mathematical representations of physical quantities that vary with position in space. They are defined as functions that assign a vector to each point in space. Vector functions can be used to describe various physical phenomena such as the velocity of an object, the force acting on an object, or the electric field in a region.\n\nA vector function $\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})$ is a function that assigns a vector $\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}) = F_1(\\mathbf{r})\\mathbf{i} + F_2(\\mathbf{r})\\mathbf{j} + F_3(\\mathbf{r})\\mathbf{k}$ to each point $\\mathbf{r} = x\\mathbf{i} + y\\mathbf{j} + z\\mathbf{k}$ in space. The components $F_1(\\mathbf{r})$, $F_2(\\mathbf{r})$, and $F_3(\\mathbf{r})$ are scalar functions that describe the magnitude and direction of the vector at each point.\n\nSome common types of vector functions include:\n\n* Position vector function $\\mathbf{r}(\\mathbf{t}) = x(\\mathbf{t})\\mathbf{i} + y(\\mathbf{t})\\mathbf{j} + z(\\mathbf{t})\\mathbf{k}$, which describes the position of an object as a function of time.\n* Velocity vector function $\\mathbf{v}(\\mathbf{t}) = \\frac{d\\mathbf{r}(\\mathbf{t})}{dt}$, which describes the velocity of an object as a function of time.\n* Acceleration vector function $\\mathbf{a}(\\mathbf{t}) = \\frac{d^2\\mathbf{r}(\\mathbf{t})}{dt^2}$, which describes the acceleration of an object as a function of time.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\n\nImagine you are on a boat in the ocean, and you want to describe the motion of the boat as it moves through the water. You can use a vector function to describe the position of the boat as a function of time. The position vector function $\\mathbf{r}(\\mathbf{t})$ would describe the position of the boat at each instant in time, taking into account the motion of the boat due to the wind, currents, and other external forces.\n\nAs the boat moves through the water, its position changes, and the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(\\mathbf{t})$ would change accordingly. The velocity vector function $\\mathbf{v}(\\mathbf{t})$ would describe the velocity of the boat at each instant in time, and the acceleration vector function $\\mathbf{a}(\\mathbf{t})$ would describe the acceleration of the boat as it moves through the water.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\n\nTo derive the velocity and acceleration vector functions, we can use the chain rule and the product rule for differentiation.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{r}(\\mathbf{t}) = x(\\mathbf{t})\\mathbf{i} + y(\\mathbf{t})\\mathbf{j} + z(\\mathbf{t})\\mathbf{k}$ be the position vector function. Then, the velocity vector function is given by:\n\n$$\\mathbf{v}(\\mathbf{t}) = \\frac{d\\mathbf{r}(\\mathbf{t})}{dt} = \\frac{dx(\\mathbf{t})}{dt}\\mathbf{i} + \\frac{dy(\\mathbf{t})}{dt}\\mathbf{j} + \\frac{dz(\\mathbf{t})}{dt}\\mathbf{k}$$\n\nUsing the chain rule, we can write:\n\n$$\\frac{dx(\\mathbf{t})}{dt} = \\frac{dx}{dt}\\frac{dt}{dt} = \\frac{dx}{dt}$$\n\n$$\\frac{dy(\\mathbf{t})}{dt} = \\frac{dy}{dt}\\frac{dt}{dt} = \\frac{dy}{dt}$$\n\n$$\\frac{dz(\\mathbf{t})}{dt} = \\frac{dz}{dt}\\frac{dt}{dt} = \\frac{dz}{dt}$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector function is given by:\n\n$$\\mathbf{v}(\\mathbf{t}) = \\frac{dx}{dt}\\mathbf{i} + \\frac{dy}{dt}\\mathbf{j} + \\frac{dz}{dt}\\mathbf{k}$$\n\nSimilarly, the acceleration vector function is given by:\n\n$$\\mathbf{a}(\\mathbf{t}) = \\frac{d^2\\mathbf{r}(\\mathbf{t})}{dt^2} = \\frac{d^2x(\\mathbf{t})}{dt^2}\\mathbf{i} + \\frac{d^2y(\\mathbf{t})}{dt^2}\\mathbf{j} + \\frac{d^2z(\\mathbf{t})}{dt^2}\\mathbf{k}"
