            "summary": "### Core Concepts\nSurface integrals are a type of integral that is used to calculate the flux of a vector field across a surface. It is defined as the dot product of the vector field and the area vector of the surface. The area vector is a vector that is perpendicular to the surface and has a magnitude equal to the area of the surface.\n\nThe surface integral of a vector field \\(\\mathbf{F}\\) over a surface \\(S\\) is denoted by $$\\iint_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.$$ The surface integral can be evaluated using a double integral, where the first integral is taken over the x and y coordinates of the surface, and the second integral is taken over the z coordinate.\n\nLine integrals, on the other hand, are used to calculate the work done by a force field along a curve. The line integral of a force field \\(\\mathbf{F}\\) along a curve \\(C\\) is denoted by $$\\int_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}.$$ The line integral can be evaluated using a single integral, where the integral is taken over the length of the curve.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\nImagine you are a sailor on a boat, and you want to calculate the amount of water that flows through a dam. The dam is a surface, and the water flowing through it is a vector field. The surface integral of the vector field over the dam will give you the total amount of water that flows through the dam. This is similar to how a surface integral calculates the flux of a vector field across a surface.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\nTo evaluate a surface integral, we need to parameterize the surface using two parameters, say \\(u\\) and \\(v\\). We can then express the surface as a function of these parameters, say \\(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)\\). The area vector of the surface can be expressed as $$\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{r}}{\\partial u} \\times \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{r}}{\\partial v} \\mathrm{d}u \\mathrm{d}v.$$\n\nThe surface integral of a vector field \\(\\mathbf{F}\\) over a surface \\(S\\) can be evaluated using the following formula:\n\n$$\\iint_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{r}}{\\partial u} \\times \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{r}}{\\partial v}\\right) \\mathrm{d}u \\mathrm{d}v.$$\n\nTo evaluate a line integral, we need to parameterize the curve using a single parameter, say \\(t\\). We can then express the curve as a function of this parameter, say \\(\\mathbf{r}(t)\\). The line integral of a force field \\(\\mathbf{F}\\) along a curve \\(C\\) can be evaluated using the following formula:\n\n$$\\int_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\mathrm{d}"
