            "topic": "Set Theory",
            "duration": 20,
            "summary": "### Core Concepts\n\nSet theory is a branch of mathematics that deals with the study of sets, which are collections of unique objects. A set is defined as a well-defined collection of distinct objects, known as elements or members, that can be anything (numbers, letters, people, etc.). The set is denoted by a capital letter, and the elements are denoted by lowercase letters. For example, if we have a set of numbers, we can write it as $A = \\\\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\\\\}$.\n\nThere are several key concepts in set theory, including:\n\n* **Subset**: A set $A$ is said to be a subset of a set $B$ if every element of $A$ is also an element of $B$. This is denoted by $A \\\\subseteq B$.\n* **Proper Subset**: A set $A$ is said to be a proper subset of a set $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B$, but there exists at least one element in $B$ that is not in $A$. This is denoted by $A \\\\subset B$.\n* **Universal Set**: The universal set is the set that contains all the elements of a given set. It is denoted by $\\\\mathbb{U}$.\n* **Empty Set**: The empty set is a set that contains no elements. It is denoted by $\\\\emptyset$.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\n\nImagine you are at a music festival, and you want to create a set of your favorite artists. You can write the set as $M = \\\\{BTS, Taylor Swift, Justin Bieber, Ariana Grande, Billie Eilish\\\\}$. In this case, the set $M$ is a collection of unique artists that you like. The set $M$ is a subset of the universal set of all artists, which is $\\\\mathbb{U} = \\\\{all artists in the world\\\\}$. The empty set $\\\\emptyset$ would be a set of artists that you don't like.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\n\n**Proof of Subset**: Let $A$ and $B$ be two sets. We want to prove that $A \\\\subseteq B$. By definition, every element of $A$ is an element of $B$. Therefore, we can write:\n\n$$A \\\\subseteq B \\\\Leftrightarrow \\\\forall x \\\\in A, x \\\\in B$$\n\n**Proof of Proper Subset**: Let $A$ and $B$ be two sets. We want to prove that $A \\\\subset B$. By definition, every element of $A$ is an element of $B$, but there exists at least one element in $B$ that is not in $A$. Therefore, we can write:\n\n$$A \\\\subset B \\\\Leftrightarrow A \\\\subseteq B \\\\land \\\\exists x \\\\in B, x \\\notin A$$\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\n\n* **Shortcut**: To prove that a set $A$ is a subset of a set $B$, you can simply show that every element of $A$ is an element of $B$.\n* **Pitfall**: Be careful when writing the subset symbol $\\\\subseteq$. Make sure to use the correct symbol, as $\\\\subset$ is used to denote a proper subset.\n* **Problem-Solving Tip**: When solving problems involving sets, try to visualize the sets as collections of objects. This will help you to better understand the relationships between the sets."
