            "summary": "### Core Concepts\nIn set theory, operations on sets refer to the various ways in which sets can be combined or manipulated to form new sets. The core concepts of set operations include:\n\n* **Union**: The union of two sets $A$ and $B$, denoted by $A \\cup B$, is the set of all elements that are in $A$, in $B$, or in both. It can be represented as $$A \\cup B = \\{x \\mid x \\in A \\text{ or } x \\in B\\}.$$\n* **Intersection**: The intersection of two sets $A$ and $B$, denoted by $A \\cap B$, is the set of all elements that are in both $A$ and $B$. It can be represented as $$A \\cap B = \\{x \\mid x \\in A \\text{ and } x \\in B\\}.$$\n* **Difference**: The difference of two sets $A$ and $B$, denoted by $A \\setminus B$ or $A - B$, is the set of all elements that are in $A$ but not in $B$. It can be represented as $$A \\setminus B = \\{x \\mid x \\in A \\text{ and } x \notin B\\}.$$\n* **Complement**: The complement of a set $A$ with respect to a universal set $U$, denoted by $A^c$ or $A'$, is the set of all elements that are in $U$ but not in $A$. It can be represented as $$A^c = \\{x \\mid x \\in U \\text{ and } x \notin A\\}"
