            "summary": "### Core Concepts\nIn set theory, a set is a well-defined collection of unique objects, known as elements or members. A set can be represented using curly brackets $\\{ \\}$, and its elements are separated by commas. For instance, the set of all even numbers can be represented as $\\{2, 4, 6, 8, \\ldots\\}$. A set can also be defined using a predicate or a property that each element must satisfy.\n\nThere are several types of sets, including:\n\n* **Finite sets**: A set with a finite number of elements, such as $\\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\\}$.\n* **Infinite sets**: A set with an infinite number of elements, such as the set of all natural numbers $\\mathbb{N} = \\{1, 2, 3, \\ldots\\}$.\n* **Empty set**: A set with no elements, denoted by $\\emptyset$.\n* **Universal set**: A set that contains all possible elements, denoted by $U$.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\nImagine a library with an infinite number of books. Each book represents an element in a set. The library itself can be thought of as the universal set, containing all possible books. A particular section of the library, such as the fiction section, can be thought of as a subset of the universal set. This analogy helps us understand how sets can be used to organize and categorize objects.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\nThe power set of a set $A$, denoted by $\\mathcal{P}(A)$, is the set of all possible subsets of $A$. For example, if $A = \\{a, b, c\\}$, then $\\mathcal{P}(A) = \\{\\emptyset, \\{a\\}, \\{b\\}, \\{c\\}, \\{a, b\\}, \\{a, c\\}, \\{b, c\\}, \\{a, b, c\\}\\}$. The power set can be calculated using the formula:\n\n$$\\mathcal{P}(A) = \\{X \\subseteq A \\mid X \\text{ is a subset of } A\\}$$\n\nThe cardinality of a set $A$, denoted by $|A|$, is the number of elements in $A$. For example, if $A = \\{a, b, c\\}$, then $|A| = 3$. The cardinality of the power set of $A$ is given by:\n\n$$|\\mathcal{P}(A)| = 2^{|A|}$$\n\nThis can be proven using the following steps:\n\n1. Let $A$ be a set with $n$ elements.\n2. Each element in $A$ can be either included or excluded from a subset of $A$.\n3. Therefore, there are $2^n$ possible subsets of $A$.\n4. Hence, $|\\mathcal{P}(A)| = 2^n = 2^{|A|}"
