            "duration": 20,
            "summary": "### Core Concepts\nFunctions are a fundamental concept in mathematics, and they are used to describe the relationship between two or more variables. A function is a relation between a set of inputs, called the domain, and a set of possible outputs, called the range. It is a rule that assigns to each input, exactly one output.\n\nIn mathematical terms, a function can be defined as:\n\n$$f: A \\to B$$\n\nwhere $A$ is the domain and $B$ is the range. The function $f$ assigns to each element $x$ in the domain $A$, an element $f(x)$ in the range $B$. This can be represented using the notation:\n\n$$f(x) = y$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the input and $y$ is the output.\n\nFunctions can be classified into several types, including:\n\n* **Injective (One-to-One)**: A function $f: A \\to B$ is injective if for every $x_1, x_2 \\in A$, $f(x_1) = f(x_2)$ implies $x_1 = x_2$.\n* **Surjective (Onto)**: A function $f: A \\to B$ is surjective if for every $y \\in B$, there exists an $x \\in A$ such that $f(x) = y$.\n* **Bijective (One-to-One Correspondence)**: A function $f: A \\to B$ is bijective if it is both injective and surjective.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\nImagine a bakery that sells cakes. The bakery has a set of ingredients, such as flour, sugar, and eggs, which are the inputs. The bakery uses these ingredients to produce a set of cakes, which are the outputs. The relationship between the ingredients and the cakes is a function. For example, if the bakery uses 2 cups of flour, 1 cup of sugar, and 3 eggs to produce a cake, then the function can be represented as:\n\n$$f(\\text{flour}, \\text{sugar}, \\text{eggs}) = \\text{cake}"
