            "topic": "Functions",
            "duration": 30,
            "summary": "### Core Concepts\n\nA function is a relation between a set of inputs (called the domain) and a set of possible outputs (called the range). It is often denoted as $f: A \\\\to B$, where $A$ is the domain and $B$ is the range. The function $f$ assigns to each element $x$ in the domain $A$ an element $f(x)$ in the range $B$. In other words, $f$ is a rule that takes an input $x$ and produces an output $f(x)$.\n\nThere are several important properties of functions:\n\n* **Injectivity**: A function $f: A \\\\to B$ is said to be injective if $f(x_1) = f(x_2)$ implies $x_1 = x_2$ for all $x_1, x_2 \\\\in A$. This means that different elements in the domain map to different elements in the range.\n* **Surjectivity**: A function $f: A \\\\to B$ is said to be surjective if for every $y \\\\in B$, there exists an $x \\\\in A$ such that $f(x) = y$. This means that every element in the range is mapped to by at least one element in the domain.\n* **Bijectivity**: A function $f: A \\\\to B$ is said to be bijective if it is both injective and surjective. This means that $f$ is a one-to-one correspondence between the domain and the range.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\n\nImagine you are a librarian and you have a collection of books on a shelf. The books represent the elements in the domain, and the authors of the books represent the elements in the range. A function $f$ would assign to each book its author. If the function is injective, then each book has a unique author, and if the function is surjective, then every author has at least one book written by them. If the function is bijective, then every book has a unique author, and every author has at least one book written by them.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\n\nTo prove that a function $f: A \\\\to B$ is injective, we need to show that $f(x_1) = f(x_2)$ implies $x_1 = x_2$ for all $x_1, x_2 \\\\in A$. Suppose that $f(x_1) = f(x_2)$ for some $x_1, x_2 \\\\in A$. Then, by the definition of a function, we have $f(x_1) = f(x_2) \\\\Rightarrow x_1 = x_2$. Therefore, $f$ is injective.\n\nTo prove that a function $f: A \\\\to B$ is surjective, we need to show that for every $y \\\\in B$, there exists an $x \\\\in A$ such that $f(x) = y$. Suppose that $y \\\\in B$. Since $f$ is a function, there exists an $x \\\\in A$ such that $f(x) = y$. Therefore, $f$ is surjective.\n\n### Exam Applications & Tips\n\nWhen solving problems involving functions, make sure to check for injectivity, surjectivity, and bijectivity. If a function is injective, then it is one-to-one, and if it is surjective, then it is onto. If a function is bijective, then it is both one-to-one and onto. Be careful when solving problems involving composite functions, as the properties of the individual functions may not be preserved. Always check the domain and range of the function, as these may affect the properties of the function.\n\nSome common exam pitfalls to watch out for include:\n\n* Assuming that a function is bijective just because it is injective or surjective.\n* Failing to check the domain and range of the function.\n* Not using the correct notation for the function, such as $f: A \\\\to B$.\n\nSome tips for solving problems involving functions include:\n\n* Start by checking the domain and range of the function.\n* Check for injectivity, surjectivity, and bijectivity.\n* Use the correct notation for the function.\n* Be careful when solving problems involving composite functions.\n* Always check your work for errors."
