            "summary": "### Core Concepts\nComposition of functions is a fundamental concept in mathematics that allows us to combine two or more functions to form a new function. Given two functions $f: A \\to B$ and $g: B \\to C$, the composition of $f$ and $g$, denoted as $(g \\circ f)(x)$ or $g(f(x))$, is a function from $A$ to $C$ defined by $$(g \\circ f)(x) = g(f(x)).$$ This means that we first apply the function $f$ to the input $x$ and then apply the function $g$ to the result.\n\nThe composition of functions is associative, meaning that for any three functions $f: A \\to B$, $g: B \\to C$, and $h: C \\to D$, we have $$(h \\circ g) \\circ f = h \\circ (g \\circ f).$$ This property allows us to compose functions in any order, as long as the output of one function matches the input of the next function.\n\nThe composition of functions is also distributive over addition, meaning that for any two functions $f: A \\to B$ and $g: A \\to B$, we have $$(f + g) \\circ h = f \\circ h + g \\circ h.$$ This property allows us to distribute the composition of functions over the addition of functions.\n\n### Real-World Analogy & Story\nImagine you are at a restaurant and you order a meal. The chef takes your order and prepares the meal according to the recipe. This is like the function $f$ that takes your order as input and produces the meal as output. However, the meal is not yet ready to be served. The waiter takes the meal from the chef and adds the finishing touches, such as garnishing and plating. This is like the function $g$ that takes the meal as input and produces the final dish as output. The composition of these two functions is like the process of ordering a meal, where the chef prepares the meal and the waiter adds the finishing touches.\n\n### Deep-Dive & Derivations\nTo prove the associativity of the composition of functions, we can use the following steps:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\n(h \\circ g) \\circ f(x) &= h(g(f(x))) \\\n&= h(f(x)) \\\n&= (h \\circ f)(x)\n\\end{align*}$$\n\nThis shows that the composition of functions is associative, meaning that we can compose functions in any order, as long as the output of one function matches the input of the next function.\n\nTo prove the distributivity of the composition of functions over addition, we can use the following steps:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\n(f + g) \\circ h(x) &= (f + g)(h(x)) \\\n&= f(h(x)) + g(h(x)) \\\n&= f \\circ h(x) + g \\circ h(x)\n\\end{align*}"
